1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,500
Two numbers limit a TCP sender: what the receiver can hold, and what the network can carry.

2
00:00:07,500 --> 00:00:15,000
The sender may have at most the smaller of the two in flight.

3
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:22,500
The receiver number is visible: a 16-bit window field in every segment header.

4
00:00:22,500 --> 00:00:30,000
The congestion window is inferred, not signalled: loss is the only feedback.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:37,500
Usable window = advertised window − bytes already in flight.

6
00:00:37,500 --> 00:00:45,000
A zero window is not the end: the sender probes, and the receiver sends a window update.

7
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:52,500
Silly window syndrome: tiny windows and tiny segments both waste the link.

8
00:00:52,500 --> 00:01:00,000
Test: with no loss at all, the advertised window alone caps the rate.

9
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:07,500
Slow start: one segment, then two, then four. It doubles every round trip.

10
00:01:07,500 --> 00:01:15,000
Per acknowledgement it is plus one segment; per round trip it is a doubling.

11
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:22,500
Doubling stops at the threshold. At or above it, growth becomes linear.

12
00:01:22,500 --> 00:01:30,000
Why exponential first? Because the sender knows nothing about the path.

13
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:37,500
Congestion avoidance: about one segment more per round trip. Additive increase.

14
00:01:37,500 --> 00:01:45,000
A timeout means severe loss: halve the threshold, restart the window at one segment.

15
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:52,500
Three duplicate ACKs are a weaker signal: retransmit at once, and cut less.

16
00:01:52,500 --> 00:02:00,000
The sawtooth: climb by one, halve, climb again.

17
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:07,500
GATE 2014 Set 1 Q27: window 32 KB at a timeout, round trip 100 ms, segment 2 KB.

18
00:02:07,500 --> 00:02:15,000
The timeout halves the threshold to 8 segments and restarts the window at 1.

19
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:22,500
Then one segment per round trip: 9, 10, 11, 12, 13, 14, 15, 16.

20
00:02:22,500 --> 00:02:30,000
Eleven round trips at 100 ms is 1100 ms. Count the trace, do not trust a formula.

21
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:37,500
GATE 2020 Q55: round trip 6 ms, receiver window 50 KB, threshold 32 KB, segment 2 KB.

22
00:02:37,500 --> 00:02:45,000
Doubling to the threshold: 2, 4, 8, 16, 32 KB after four round trips.

23
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:52,500
Then two kilobytes per round trip for the remaining six: up to 44 KB.

24
00:02:52,500 --> 00:03:00,000
Change one number and the answer changes: the threshold is given, not derived.

25
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:07,500
GATE 2024 Set 2 Q54: window 12 MSS at a timeout, two clean round trips after.

26
00:03:07,500 --> 00:03:15,000
Watch the cap: doubling stops at the threshold, so 4 would become 6, not 8.

27
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:22,500
Had the loss been three duplicate ACKs, the window would be 6, then 7, then 8.

28
00:03:22,500 --> 00:03:30,000
Same numbers, two answers: 4 MSS after a timeout, 8 MSS after duplicate ACKs.

29
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:37,500
Average throughput of AIMD = three quarters of the peak window, over the round trip.

30
00:03:37,500 --> 00:03:45,000
Worked: peak 100 segments of 1460 bytes, round trip 40 ms, gives 21.9 Mbps.

31
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:52,500
A window that is too small leaves the pipe empty: fill it with bandwidth × round trip.

32
00:03:52,500 --> 00:04:00,000
So the 16-bit window is scaled by an option, and the arithmetic is unchanged.

33
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:07,500
The socket calls in order: the server binds, listens, accepts; the client connects.

34
00:04:07,500 --> 00:04:15,000
The handshake maps onto the calls: SYN, SYN-ACK, then ACK.

35
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:22,500
Segment size follows from the link: MSS = MTU − 40 for IPv4 TCP.

36
00:04:22,500 --> 00:04:30,000
Closing is not symmetric: the active closer waits in TIME_WAIT for two segment lifetimes.

37
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:37,500
For a window question: convert to segments, then walk round trip by round trip.

38
00:04:37,500 --> 00:04:45,000
Four slips account for most lost marks in this clause.

39
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:52,500
Two questions separate the hard cases: which window binds, and which signal fired.

40
00:04:52,500 --> 00:05:00,000
This clause connects back to IP and forward to DNS and HTTP.

